# Two-factor authentication backend TODO

Generated by the light-it auth package. `auth:setup` wrote every 2FA stub,
action and route, but it cannot edit your boilerplate's own login - that
file is not generated by this package, so nothing here rewrites it
automatically.

## One line to paste

Open `LoginAction::execute()` and paste this line right after the host
login's `$request->session()->regenerate();` and before `return $user;`:

```php
{{gateSnippet}}
```

The gate tears the just-created session back down itself before throwing a
challenge, so the paste point only matters in that it must run after
`attempt()`/`regenerate()` has committed the session and before the user is
returned - anywhere in that window works.

Every alternative login path this package generates (OTP today; Google SSO
and passkeys as they land) already routes through the same gate via
`LoginByUserAction::execute()`. This is the only manual step left to gate
your own native login the same way.

Once pasted, a user with 2FA configured gets a `200` with
`token_type: "verification_required"` (or `"setup_required"` if 2FA is
mandatory and they haven't set it up yet) instead of a session on login.
Completing the challenge - the one-time password or a recovery code -
creates the session and returns the same `UserResource` a normal login does.

Delete this file once you've pasted the line.

### Optional: avoid the `Login` event side effect

`$guard->attempt()` fires Laravel's `Login` event even for a user who is about
to be challenged. If your listeners on that event should only run for a fully
authenticated session, swap `attempt()` for `validate()` + the gate + `login()`:

```php
if (! $guard->validate($credentials)) {
    throw new UnauthenticatedException;
}

/** @var User $user */
$user = $guard->getLastAttempted();

app(TwoFactorLoginGate::class)->guardAgainstChallenge($user);
$guard->login($user);
```

`validate()` checks the credentials without logging the user in, so the gate
runs first and the session is only ever established once the challenge (if
any) is cleared - `login()` here does what `attempt()` used to.
